Both runs loaded Linux, validated their unique nonce marker, and reached the
expected budget stop at retired=2,999,999,998. The series outcome is
`passed` for the smoke scenario, but its run profile is `smoke_not_acceptance`
and runner acceptance is `diagnostic_not_counted`.

Automatic-series comparison
---------------------------
The strict comparison returned matches=false, explained=1,
unexplained=2:
- run[1].machine_observations differs
- run[1].ordered_uart_bytes differs after nonce normalization

The comparator correctly did not apply the counted Stage 4 nonce volatile
projection to this diagnostic profile. Raw machine observations include
different terminal registers and counters. IRQ0 edges were 34,741/34,742,
delivered IRQs 34,746/34,747, quanta 34,506,214/34,507,731, total CPL0 RDTSC
reads 34,380,601/34,382,096, and #PF counts 33/33. At the selected delay_tsc
RIP, counts were 33,988,924/33,990,416 (delta 1,492).

The two schema-v7 sidecars retained 66,384 and 66,387 stride samples, with no
omitted candidates. All 66,384 shared hit ordinals were compared. The
requested-cycle value differs at one shared sample, ordinal 994,304
(0xdbb7b/0xf4217); elapsed_cycles differs at 64,450 shared samples. Using
the derived per-sample predicate elapsed_cycles >= requested_cycles, 117
shared samples have different outcomes. The first differing sampled outcome
is ordinal 1,255,424: run 0001 is below the requested interval
(0x872ab < 0xf4217), while run 0002 is above it (0xf44eb >= 0xf4217).
This is the first differing sampled predicate at ordinal 1,255,424, not a
bound on the first divergence: unsampled hits may differ earlier, and equal
global hit ordinals do not align individual delay invocations. The trace
establishes sampled threshold-path variation but does not identify its cause
or classify it as benign.

UART files are both 23,356 bytes. The official comparator reports a strict
post-nonce difference. As an exploratory check only, additionally removing
the leading printk timestamp from each line leaves two payload differences:
available memory 62,024/61,972 KiB and sched_clock calibration values. This
extra transformation is not the comparator's acceptance rule.

Same-input replay of run 0001
----------------------------
Run 0001 was repeated as a direct diagnostic run with the same nonce
`OD518e8975553f95858000040f800000000`, same CPU/boot options, budget and
artifact hashes. The replay loaded Linux and retired 2,999,999,998
instructions. Input metadata and guest command line matched. Its complete
machine stop object (excluding the separately checked inline UART bytes),
UART bytes, and schema-v7 observation sidecar were byte-identical to run
0001. This demonstrates repeatability for that same guest-visible input on
this host/build. It does not prove that changing the nonce caused the
cross-run differences, explain those differences, or turn the unique-nonce
series into acceptance evidence.

The direct replay used `--nonce OD518e8975553f95858000040f800000000`, the
same input paths/hashes and boot flags shown above, `--budget 3000000000`,
and `--execution-observations-out
work/stage5/stage5-s5-07-rdtsc-delay-stride512-same-input-run1-replay-20261116/execution-observations.json`
with the same selected RIP and stride. Its runner JSON was written to
`work/stage5/stage5-s5-07-rdtsc-delay-stride512-same-input-run1-replay-20261116/runner.json`.

Run 0002 was then repeated the same way, with nonce
`OD518e898753551a790000040f800000001` and output directory
`work/stage5/stage5-s5-07-rdtsc-delay-stride512-same-input-run2-replay-20261116/`.
It loaded and retired 2,999,999,998 instructions. The source and replay had
equal nonce, inputs, command line, complete stop object (including inline
UART), UART bytes, and schema-v7 sidecar bytes. The selected sidecar hash was
`4a4450fa2aeb860282cd0154a426b36151ff770a5011006fe37658ee13a166a0` in
both files; replay UART SHA-256 was
`045dbefb9eb7fa0121f9fb9b64a7f1587063b5219551fd305bc3202469c8b924`, and
replay runner JSON SHA-256 was
`caf763871f2b68d59a7b2fd009b0d6407a2a527e2fffdfe550617bdc9610bdec`.

Both unique-nonce source runs therefore reproduce exactly under their own
recorded guest inputs on this host/build. The only recorded guest-visible
input difference between the source runs is the nonce value in `od.nonce=`.
This is consistent with deterministic nonce-conditioned behavior, but does
not establish a mechanism or make the cross-run machine/UART differences
benign. The series still fails strict comparison and is not acceptance
evidence.

